  "status": "published",
  "title": "Trump Proposes Tariffs on 14 Trading Partners. Which U.S. Factories Would Pay More?",
  "deck": "Washington says the duties would counter foreign policies that encourage factories to produce more than markets can absorb. U.S. companies that buy covered parts and materials from abroad could face higher costs.",
  "category": "economy",
  "categoryLabel": "Economy & Energy",
  "type": "news",
  "author": "EP News",
  "publishedAt": "2026-11-25T16:48:02.667Z",
  "updatedAt": "2026-11-25T16:48:02.667Z",
  "body": [
    "The Trump administration proposed tariffs on imports from 14 trading partners on Wednesday, including China, the European Union, Japan and South Korea. U.S. Trade Representative Jamieson Greer said policies such as preferential financing and production incentives have helped sustain excess manufacturing capacity overseas, putting U.S. producers at a disadvantage.",
    "The proposal could give U.S. factories that compete with those imports more room to win orders. It could also raise costs for American factories that buy the affected goods to make their own products. A manufacturer competing against imported machinery and a manufacturer buying imported components can face opposite consequences from the same tariff schedule.",
    "The proposed rates depend on the product and the country",
    "USTR proposed up to 20% on many Chinese goods, with a lower rate or exemptions for certain products. For the EU, the agency proposed a 15% rate that accounts for normal U.S. duties and existing Section 301 tariffs, with exemptions. That calculation matters to importers because it can leave considerably less than 15 percentage points in new duties on a particular shipment.",
    "Vietnam faces a proposed rate of up to 35%, net of existing Section 301 tariffs and subject to exemptions. USTR also laid out a path to a 20% rate if Vietnam reaches an agreement that addresses the agency's findings. Reuters reported that existing and pending trade deals could limit what importers ultimately pay.",
    "USTR opened 16 investigations in March under Section 301 of the Trade Act of 1974. It issued findings for 14 economies on Wednesday and left its decisions on Mexico and Norway pending.",
    "Earlier tariffs show where costs can land",
    "American importers pay duties when covered goods enter the United States. A factory that imports a covered part can absorb the extra cost, ask its supplier for a lower price, look for another source or pass some of the cost to customers. How much it can pass on depends on its contracts, its margins and what competitors charge.",
    "A 2023 U.S. International Trade Commission study found that U.S. importers bore nearly all the cost of the 2018–2021 tariffs it examined. The commission estimated that the earlier Section 301 duties on China cut imports of affected goods by 13% and raised the value of U.S. production of those goods by 0.4% on average. The study did not estimate costs for downstream industries using goods covered by those Section 301 tariffs, so those figures give only part of the picture for manufacturers buying imported inputs.",
    "USTR is accepting written comments through January 5, 2027, and plans to begin hearings on January 25. The November announcement sets out proposed rates and exemptions. The final schedules will determine which U.S. factories gain protection from imports and which have to recalculate the cost of their parts."
  ],
